      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-11013",
      ":question/problem": "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"
    },
